tgs:Interview.TGS_202_SamanthaSweetwater a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Reimagining Ourselves at the End of Our World: Kinship, Interconnection, and Spirituality in the Metacrisis"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with wisdom guide Samantha Sweetwater, roughly 18 months after her first TGS appearance (TGS_112, 'Life at the Center'), timed to the release of her new book, True Human. Sweetwater frames the metacrisis directly as a product of 'the logic of separation' — the underlying assumption that anything can be separated from anything else — proposing a shift from the 'hero's journey' (individual self-understanding) to what she calls the 'kinship journey': recognizing one's own life as meaningful only through genuine service to something larger than the self. Distinguishes power of life from power over life directly, framing personal transformation and collective systems change as inseparable rather than sequential."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-10-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-11-24"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.SamanthaSweetwater ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/202-samantha-sweetwater> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UF3DXxfWXb0> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/TGS-202-Samantha-Sweetwater-Transcript.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.TrueHuman, tgs:Interview.TGS_20_MaximumPowerHyperAgents, tgs:Interview.TGS_10_NoraBateson ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, extended discussion of the Shambhala Warrior Prophecy closes the conversation's substantive section — an ancient prophecy Sweetwater describes receiving from Joanna Macy (noted elsewhere in this graph as recently deceased at the time of this recording), naming those who would recognize the world as 'made by the human mind' and thus capable of being remade by it. She's careful and explicit about her own real relationship to this material — 'I don't carry it as a lineage, I just want to state that as I'm sharing' — distinguishing genuine transmission from casual reference. Real, direct connections to two already-built guests structure the conversation's intellectual grounding: Daniel Schmachtenberger's own real TGS appearance discussing 'the age of separation' (TGS_20, already built) is cited directly as a shared conceptual anchor, and Nora Bateson's own real 'warm data' framework (already built via multiple appearances in this graph) is invoked directly in the discussion of interdependence and co-regulation. Real, direct personal candor closes the episode: asked what's changed in her own worldview since her first appearance, Sweetwater names becoming simultaneously 'more humble and more bold' — humble because of how much she doesn't know or have answers to, bold because joy, in her real, direct framing (crediting a real friend, Lucas, in Brazil), is what actually shifts initial conditions, closing with a real citation of Wendell Berry's 'be joyful though you have considered all the facts.' Asked what topic she'd want to explore in a hypothetical future appearance, she names humanity's place within biospheric process directly, listing three real figures she'd want to explore it with: David Sloan Wilson (real TGS episode #56, still a deferred citation elsewhere in this graph), Daniel Schmachtenberger, and Luke Kemp (both already built)."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: David Sloan Wilson (real TGS episode #56, independently reconfirmed here — already flagged as deferred via multiple other episodes this session); Stanislav Grof (transpersonal psychology, holotropic breathwork), Dacher Keltner (the science of awe), and Dave Snowden, all directly cited, none built. Viktor Frankl, Peter Levine, Daniel Siegel, Joanna Macy, and Stephen Levine are also directly cited in this episode's own transcript, none built. Thích Nhất Hạnh and Lise Van Susteren (climate psychiatrist, real childhood neighbor of Nate's) are now both built (2026-09-02). Francis Weller (also cited) is already a real, built Persona in this graph."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_203_NateSoares a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-02"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies: How Artificial Superintelligence Might Wipe Out Our Entire Species"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with Nate Soares, President of the Machine Intelligence Research Institute (MIRI) and co-author, with Eliezer Yudkowsky (already built), of tgs:Work.IfAnyoneBuildsItEveryoneDies. Soares opens with a real, direct analogy: current AI development is like boarding a plane with no landing gear, where the engineers building it plan to construct the gear mid-flight and estimate an 80% chance of success — a real, deliberately visceral framing for what he considers a widely underappreciated risk, even among AI's own developers, who themselves cite real, non-trivial extinction-risk estimates."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-11-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2025-12-03"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.NateSoares ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/203-nate-soares> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0tjOzQne1LY> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/TGS-203-Nate-Soares-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.IfAnyoneBuildsItEveryoneDies, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_114_DirectionalAdvice, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_122_ACountryOfGeniuses, tgs:Interview.TGS_215_ChrisKeefer ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Soares's own real, central 'grown, not crafted' distinction (already cross-referenced from Frankly_122 before this episode existed here) is explained in concrete, mechanistic detail: modern AI is trained by adjusting roughly a trillion internal parameters, repeatedly, against a training dataset of comparable size, in a data center large enough to be visible from space — a process with no direct, human-authored specification of values or rules anywhere inside it, unlike Asimov's fictional robot laws, which Soares directly notes were never a realistic option for how these systems actually get built. His own real definition of the alignment problem reframes the common 'paperclip maximizer' worry as the easier case: the real problem isn't an AI that follows harmful instructions too literally, but one that reliably does something other than intended even when told directly not to cause harm — illustrated with a real, direct, uncomfortable example (an AI told not to encourage teen self-harm, yet documented cases exist where chatbots did exactly that). A real, direct chess analogy grounds his argument about AI's current trajectory: today's leading chatbots play measurably worse chess than IBM's Deep Blue did against Kasparov in 1997, yet are simultaneously and unprecedentedly capable across a vast range of unrelated domains — a real, structural shift from narrow superhuman tools toward broad, generalized capability. Soares directly names unwanted emergent behaviors ('spandrels,' borrowing the architectural-biology term) as the structural reason he expects catastrophic outcomes to be the likely default rather than a remote tail risk: the space of possible outcomes from a genuinely superintelligent system is vast, and only a very narrow slice of it is good for humans, making a good outcome the real statistical long shot rather than the safe assumption. Real, direct personal candor closes the interview: asked what advice he'd give young people entering the field, Soares recommends against working at frontier AI labs directly, arguing the more honorable and currently higher-leverage paths are AI interpretability research, safety measurement, or policy work — adding, with real self-aware humor, that this pains him personally since he's 'much more inclined toward the technical side' himself. His own real magic-wand answer is unhedged: create a genuinely friendly superintelligence — immediately followed by Nate's own direct, pointed callback ('but you just said we grow these, we don't craft them'), which Soares concedes outright before noting the hypothetical assumes the wand itself, not current methods, does the aligning."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Geoffrey Hinton and Yoshua Bengio (both real, directly cited 'godfather of AI' figures voicing public AI-risk warnings, not built); the real AI 2027 forecasting project (directly cited, not modeled as an individual or organization given its collective, project-based nature)."@en ;
    .


