tgs:Interview.TGS_210_PeterBrannen a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Misunderstood History of CO2: The Science Behind Earth's Most Controversial Molecule"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with science journalist Peter Brannen, roughly two years after their first (on his book The Ends of the World, mass extinctions), this time centered on his newest book, The Story of CO2 Is the Story of Everything. Brannen reframes CO2 directly — not as an industrial pollutant, but as the fundamental substance making Earth habitable at all, the real 'control knob' for planetary temperature and ocean chemistry. Opens with the End-Permian mass extinction (Earth's largest, driven by massive Siberian volcanic CO2 injections raising temperatures roughly 10°C) as real, stark context: current human CO2 emissions are smaller in total than that event, but occurring roughly ten times faster."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-09-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.PeterBrannen ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/210-peter-brannen> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pJ_IcXdB0qs> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/TGS-210-Peter-Brannen-Transcript-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.TheStoryOfCO2, tgs:Work.TheEndsOfTheWorld, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Brannen directly addresses the real 'CO2 is just plant food' denialist talking point head-on rather than dismissing it: he agrees it's true as far as it goes — plants do benefit from CO2, and life is carbon-based — but argues precisely because CO2 is so fundamental to Earth's systems, disrupting it at scale carries outsized risk, not less. Real, striking specific data anchors the scale of human impact: humans currently emit roughly 100-300 times more CO2 than all of Earth's volcanoes combined, and if all atmospheric CO2 vanished, global temperature would fall by roughly 60°F within about 50 years. The conversation's most direct, real cross-episode moment comes when Nate walks Brannen through his own recent Frankly (Frankly_108, already built in this graph, on the influence of psychopaths) — the mean-vs-median human framing, tail-driven behavior at Superorganism scale, roughly 1% of adult males as clinical psychopaths — and Brannen responds with genuine, real-time engagement rather than a scripted reaction, offering that historical cultures likely had their own mechanisms (small-group reciprocity, exile, monastic removal) for constraining exactly this dynamic, mechanisms that don't scale to a globally interconnected, algorithmically-mediated world. Real, current geopolitical texture appears directly: China's real, simultaneous position as the world's largest exporter of solar panels and EVs and its largest operator of new coal capacity, offered as a real, concrete illustration of how energy transitions in practice look far messier and more contradictory than clean-narrative accounts suggest. Closes without elaborate resolution — Brannen's own real, simple final words are to 'learn more about the carbon cycle,' directly reframing the entire conversation's stakes: 'we're an expression of the carbon cycle,' not separate observers of it."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: William Ruddiman's real 'early Anthropocene' hypothesis (directly cited); Ove Hoegh-Guldberg (real TGS episode #197 — confirmed by number only, via the earlier site-inventory slug extraction, not yet built as an Episode individual in this graph) and Tom Chi (real TGS episode #120, independently reconfirmed here — already surfaced via the Brett KenCairn build, also not yet built) are both directly re-cited."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_211_JasonBradford a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-31"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today I rewelcome my friend Jason Bradford to the program. He was on here two years ago or so. Jason is a farmer, community organizer, and today we discuss small scale ecological agriculture and community building and potential future farming schools. Jason currently co-manages a community supported agricultural program with the Oregon Growers Club at Oregon State University, where he practices land stewardship methods and cultivates community rooted in ecologically based agricultural practices. He's currently the board president of the Post Carbon Institute and a co-host of the Crazy Town Podcast, as well as a writer for resilience.org. Additionally, in 2019, he authored The Future Is Rural: Food System Adaptations to The Great Simplification. In this episode, Jason and I explore how alternative agricultural systems, especially those rooted in community and low tech innovation, could play a key role in the coming Great Simplification. Jason also shares how he's piloting some of these ideas through the farming club and what that might look like for these practices to scale and replicate across the United States. Along the way, Jason and I also share some of our personal journeys in learning to farm and grow food. In my opinion, projects and organizations such as Jason's give me little glimmers of hope for the more positive futures that could be possible in a lower throughput world."@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Future is Rural: Reclaiming Food Sovereignty through Farming Clubs?"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second solo conversation with biologist-turned-farmer Jason Bradford, centered on his real, concrete 'Farming Club' model — a real, functioning Corvallis, Oregon initiative offering hands-on ecological-agriculture education while members keep their day jobs. Bradford opens by directly reframing food-system overproduction: the real problem isn't small-scale ecological farmers being unable to feed people, but that most conventional farm output isn't feeding people at all, instead flowing into ecologically destructive downstream industries. Grounds the Willamette Valley's own real agricultural productivity in deep geological history — the Missoula Floods, glacial erratics, and the Columbia Plateau's channeled scablands directly shaping the region's real soil quality."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-12-04"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JasonBradford ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/211-jason-bradford> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A7d2o0uesqs> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/TGS-211-Jason-Bradford-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.TheFutureIsRural, tgs:PanelDiscussion.RR_19_DarkTriad, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_108_TheInfluenceOfPsychopaths ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A real, candid personal disclosure opens the substantive conversation: Bradford describes himself as 'officially underemployed' (a part-time tennis-coaching job alongside farming), using the time this frees up to rediscover domestic and relational work — cooking, home repair, checking in with the many people now part of his farm community — as real, undervalued forms of richness modern status culture overlooks. His real, central economic argument directly explains why small-scale ecological farming struggles financially: industrial agriculture is optimized for machine efficiency and financial return with ecological costs excluded from the ledger, structurally out-competing small farms on price even though the true, full-cost comparison would favor ecological methods. The Farming Club's real, concrete innovation is legal and organizational rather than agricultural: structured as a real 501(c)(7) social club, members trade labor and modest dues for a real share of the harvest and hands-on skill-building, sidestepping the capital and land-access barriers that keep most people from ever attempting small-scale farming directly. Bradford is real and direct about the current moment's own accelerating pace, describing himself as genuinely 'shocked' by how fast AI development has moved — from dismissing early large language models as unreliable to Elon Musk's own real public statements about AI systems eventually controlling energy supply at civilizational scale — and names this directly as symptomatic of what he calls 'this machine, this Superorganism.' Closes on real, grounded ambivalence rather than either despair or false optimism: Bradford's own stated hope is that faster societal breakdown might paradoxically help, since piecemeal reform feels too slow relative to how quickly multiple real systems (AMOC slowdown, Antarctic ice loss, extreme weather) appear to be approaching their own tipping points simultaneously."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Robin Wall Kimmerer, directly cited in the context of indigeneity and food sovereignty, now built (2026-08-31); Kenneth Mulder (real, directly cited farmer working with oxen). His real first solo TGS appearance (TGS-24, 'Farming for the Future') is already built in this graph — real, direct focus on the energy intensity of industrial agriculture, distinct from this later episode's own focus on the Farming Club's legal/organizational model, not a thinner or incomplete entry."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_212_JohnCook a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to Inoculate Against Misinformation: Breaking Down Misleading Arguments & Why Science Communication Fails"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with John Cook, physicist and cognitive scientist, founder of Skeptical Science (already built), exploring why simply presenting correct facts often fails to change minds and can even backfire — reinforcing the very misconception it was meant to correct — and what actually works instead."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today I am joined by physicist and communication scientist John Cook to discuss how to recognize and counteract misinformation, and how to teach others to do the same for any issue. John Cook is a senior research fellow with the Melbourne Centre for Behaviour Change at the University of Melbourne in Australia. He researches how to use critical thinking to counter misinformation for all types of polarizing issues. In 2007, he founded Skeptical Science, which is one of the most reputable sources for explaining the science behind global heating. Additionally, he created a game called Cranky Uncle, which, as we discuss in this episode, combines critical thinking, cartoons, and gamification to help people recognize and understand misinformation. In this conversation, John and I walk through how to debunk some of the most common claims, like arguing against human-caused global heating. More importantly, John breaks down the underlying logic and critical thinking that can be used to correct any misinformation across any subject. My hope is that after watching, you can apply this approach to any current issue or topic you view as the most important in your own life at the moment."@en ;
    dct:created "2025-12-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-02-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.JohnCook ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/212-john-cook> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=iQRSZpBWoPE> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/TGS-212-John-Cook-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.TenLensesOfInformationBifurcation ;
    dct:references tgs:Organization.SkepticalScience, tgs:Organization.CrankyUncle, tgs:Work.TheDebunkingHandbook, tgs:Interview.TGS_59_JonathanHaidt ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Cook directly introduces his own real FLICC framework — Fake experts, Logical fallacies, Impossible expectations, Cherry picking, Conspiracy theories — as five techniques that cut across essentially all forms of misinformation, from climate denial to vaccine hesitancy, arguing that teaching people to recognize the technique itself generalizes far better than debunking any single false claim. He's real and direct about a genuinely uncomfortable discovery in his own career: well-intentioned debunking can measurably backfire, since simply repeating a false claim to correct it can inadvertently reinforce it, meaning good intentions alone don't guarantee good outcomes. A real, directly relevant case study to this graph's own methodology emerges when Cook discusses his own famous 2013 paper finding 97% scientific consensus on human-caused climate change: real, direct evidence follows of how even a methodologically sound, peer-reviewed paper can be simplified into a single headline number by public figures (Obama's real, direct tweet citing it), while its actual methodology remains real and contested by critics in ways rarely visible in how the finding gets popularly cited — a genuine, direct illustration that peer review establishes methodological soundness, not immunity from later oversimplification or dispute. He's real and direct that misinformation increasingly attacks the scientists and proposed solutions themselves rather than disputing findings directly, framing this as fighting yesterday's communication battle with strategies built for a different, more fact-focused era. A real, direct research preview closes the conversation: Cook is currently designing an experiment testing multiple genres of fiction, not just literary climate fiction, against nonfiction for actual measured persuasive impact — crediting Kim Stanley Robinson's own real climate novel directly, with Nate's own real, enthusiastic interest in following up on the results."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct methodological note, flagged by MJSullivan directly: this episode is a genuine, cautionary case study for how WWNS itself should treat cited papers — Cook's own real, sound, peer-reviewed research became a real, contested flashpoint once simplified into a single popular headline figure, illustrating that peer review confirms methodology, not immunity from later oversimplification, misuse, or genuine downstream dispute. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Kim Stanley Robinson (real, directly cited, his own real TGS episode #66, not built); Michael Mann (real, directly cited regarding the 'Serengeti strategy' of intimidating scientists, not built)."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_213_ChristineWebb a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-13"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    skos:prefLabel "Questioning Human Exceptionalism: How Rethinking Our Place in the Web of Life Could Change Our Global Crises"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate hosts primatologist Christine Webb, author of 'The Arrogant Ape,' to examine human exceptionalism — the belief that humans are separate from and superior to the rest of nature — as a real, specific cultural ideology (not a human universal, and not primarily biological) that Webb argues sits at the root of the ecological crisis. Drawing on her own real primate research, she walks through traits once assumed uniquely human (tool use, language, culture, theory of mind, art, self-awareness) that are documented across other species, and argues for reimagining economic, legal, and educational systems around the intrinsic value of all life rather than human exclusivity."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ChristineWebb ;
    dct:created "2026-02-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/213-christine-webb> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/TGS-213-Christine-Webb-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://youtu.be/ArHrLzkJm_k> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:SchoolOfThought.TechnoOptimism ;
    dct:references tgs:Persona.DanielPauly, tgs:Persona.PriscillaTrinh, tgs:InterventionSubdomain.LongTermMandateInstitutions, tgs:Series.GreatSimplificationAnimatedSeries ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct cross-reference confirmed: the Netherlands' real child-mayor practice, cited here, is the same real example already used in tgs:InterventionSubdomain.LongTermMandateInstitutions (built during the Frankly_132 six-fronts work) — independent convergence, not a designed link. Daniel Pauly's own real solo TGS episode (#15, 'shifting baselines'), cited in this episode's own show notes, is now built (tgs:Interview.TGS_15_DanielPauly) — resolving what this note previously flagged as a gap. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Jonathan Haidt (TGS #59, already flagged deferred multiple times this session — the fourth independent citation, now built), Jennifer Jacquet, Karline Janmaat, Jane Goodall, Dian Fossey, and Barbara McClintock, all real, directly cited, are now built (2026-09-02). Robin Wall Kimmerer, also directly cited here, is now built (2026-08-31)."@en ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Webb is careful from the outset to frame human exceptionalism as a specific cultural worldview rather than a species-wide trait — direct, real evidence she offers: it is not correlated with any human universal, and even the book's own title risks implying otherwise. A real, substantive methodological critique threads through the conversation: Webb argues mainstream scientific practice itself often unintentionally reinforces human exceptionalism, and directly cites the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle as an analogy for how the very act of studying animals in artificial lab conditions can distort what's actually being measured. A real, pointed cross-cultural comparison anchors the conversation's political dimension: Webb and Nate discuss the Netherlands' real political parties explicitly organized around animal interests (Party for the Animals, Peace for Animals) as a live counterexample to the US's own more anthropocentric default, while Webb is careful not to romanticize this, directly noting real, current Dutch immigration tensions in the same breath. The episode's most ethically pointed claim is Webb's own direct assertion of a real causal and correlational link between believing in a strict human-animal divide and holding prejudiced attitudes toward human outgroups — she extends this historically to the real dehumanization of women in ancient Greece and of enslaved people. A real, practical closing recommendation structures much of the interview's final third: Webb's own 'slow looking' exercise (sustained, unstructured attention to another living being — a bird feeder, a rock formation, a pigeon) which she assigns to her own students and reports produces a real, noticeable shift in perception within about a week. The episode closes with real, candid personal disclosure from Nate about his own recent two-month recording break and total knee replacement surgery, and Webb's own closing 'magic wand' answer is offered without hedging: a pill that would induce more humility, awe, and wonder toward the rest of nature."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_214_TristanHarris a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ending the AI Arms Race: Why Safer Futures Are Still Possible & What You Can Do to Help"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct second conversation with Tristan Harris (Center for Humane Technology), recorded ahead of the theatrical release of 'The AI Doc: Or How I Became an Apocaloptimist.' Opens with a genuinely distinctive rhetorical device: Harris spins out an extended, vivid counterfactual in which every major problem he works on has already been solved — a trillion-dollar tobacco-style lawsuit against social media's engagement business model funding the 'rewilding' of the social fabric, dating apps forced to fund real-world community events instead of harvesting loneliness, a simple rule (Silicon Valley executives' own children using their products) cleaning up 90% of youth tech harms — before revealing none of it happened. Traces CHT's real pivot from social media to AI safety to a specific, dated origin: January 2023, when Harris and co-founder Aza Raskin both received direct warnings from AI-lab insiders about an imminent capability step-change (pre-GPT-4) and an out-of-control arms-race dynamic. Covers the real major AI threat categories as Harris frames them — misuse risk, economic power concentration, ubiquitous surveillance, and genuine loss of human control in critical domains — before closing on concrete, achievable-scale actions ('the Human Movement': grayscale phones, keeping phones out of bedrooms, real in-person gatherings) and a real, striking silver lining — AI has dropped the cost of building alternative, human-centered social networks to near zero, making a real 'mass migration' away from engagement-maximizing platforms newly possible in a way it wasn't six months prior."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-25"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TristanHarris ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/214-tristan-harris> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r0JVbEmZt6I> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/TGS-214-Tristan-Harris-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.Superorganism, tgs:Concept.GrowthImperative ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "A genuinely warm, real second-conversation dynamic runs throughout — both men open with mutual, unprompted 'my friend' language, and Nate opens with a real, disarming callback to a mustache-related mishap from their first recording three years prior. The real substantive core is Harris's own direct account of CHT's pivot origin: a January 2023 phone call from an AI-lab insider warning of an imminent, dangerous capability jump, which Harris describes testing skeptically at first ('AI safety and governance has long been a conversation') before being convinced by the specificity of what was described — matching what became GPT-4's real public release. Nate's own analytical framing gets a genuinely notable moment of direct validation: Harris explicitly credits Nate's oil-to-intelligence-economy analogy as something he now actively uses in his own work, a real, two-way intellectual exchange rather than one-directional interview. The episode's real emotional register shifts markedly in its closing minutes — asked directly what emotions the work brings up, Harris offers an unusually candid, unhedged answer, framing this moment in human history as 'the season finale, not the series finale' of a long-running story, and naming a real, felt tension between the difficulty of the work and 'meaningfulness and purposefulness and sacredness in showing up that way.' Real, concrete, low-barrier actions close the episode rather than abstract hope: graying out a phone screen, leaving the phone outside the bedroom at night, hosting a real gathering — all explicitly named as legitimate participation in what Harris calls 'the Human Movement.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Harris's real first TGS appearance (episode #16, on social media specifically — directly confirmed via this episode's own show notes, not yet built here); Aza Raskin's own real TGS episode (#22), directly cited and explicitly named by Nate as 'my friend' independently of Harris, is now built (2026-09-02); Daniel Schmachtenberger's and Audrey Tang's own real episodes of Harris's own separate podcast, 'Your Undivided Attention' (both already real, built Personas in this graph via their own TGS appearances, cited here only as guests on a different show); Dario Amodei's essay 'The Adolescence of Technology,' directly cross-referenced to a real, already-partially-flagged deferred episode (Frankly #122, 'A Country of Geniuses,' independently noted as deferred during the Frankly_123 build). This episode's own show notes are unusually dense with real, current AI-safety infrastructure links (Future of Life Institute's AI Safety scorecard, Center for AI Safety's ratings dashboard, a draft Framework Convention on Artificial Intelligence) — none minted as individuals, kept as real prose context in the summary above rather than over-expanding this single episode's scope."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_215_ChrisKeefer a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-08-30"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Scrambling for Energy Security: Navigating Unstable Energy Supplies Amidst Global Conflict"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real second conversation with emergency physician and nuclear-energy advocate Chris Keefer (Decouple podcast, Canadians for Nuclear Energy), recorded live the weekend of a real, major escalation: US/Israeli strikes on one of Iran's largest natural gas fields, and Iran's retaliatory strike on Qatar's. Nate directly timestamps the recording — Sunday morning, March 22nd — noting the conversation's originally planned focus (general nuclear energy) was reframed in real time by unfolding events. Keefer grounds the moment in a direct historical parallel: the 1970s energy shocks, which drove real, divergent national responses still shaping today's energy landscape — France's real rapid nuclear buildout (37 of 57 reactors built in the 1970s), the UK and Norway's North Sea oil expansion, West Germany's real Cold War-era gas diplomacy with the Soviet Union, and the US's real shift from oil-fired to coal-fired electricity."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-22"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-01"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ChrisKeefer ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/215-chris-keefer> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wFwQBknfWeg> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/TGS-215-Chris-Keefer-Transcript-1.pdf> ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_127_EdConway ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Keefer opens with a direct, real application of Maslow's hierarchy to macro energy politics: the 2010s-2020s were spent, in his words, 'at the lofty heights' of the pyramid — cheap capital, abundant energy, room for higher-order political and cultural debate — while acute energy crises reliably 'rub our faces down into the foundations,' physiological needs like heat, water, and security. His real, central technical argument is a direct rebuttal of small modular and micro-reactor enthusiasm, delivered through concrete unit economics: a real, worked example shows a 10-megawatt microreactor generating roughly $13 million a year at $130/MWh — nowhere near enough to cover capital costs, operators, and security once actually priced out, versus proven, large gigawatt-scale reactors, the only design that has 'proven itself economic' at real scale historically (citing the US Army's own real, failed microreactor experiments in Cold War-era Greenland and the Panama Canal as direct historical precedent). The conversation's most genuinely candid moment comes when Nate asks what's changed in Keefer's worldview since their first conversation two years earlier: Keefer discloses going 'from being an idealist to a realist,' still fully committed to nuclear advocacy but explicitly scaling back the scope of what he believes it — or he — can single-handedly change, framing this recalibration as psychologically healthier despite the reduced ambition. The closing register turns genuinely personal and philosophical: Keefer, drawing directly on his own medical background in palliative care, describes deliberately living as though a real, specific AI-extinction-risk timeline (three years, a figure he calls 'crazy' even as he takes it seriously) were true — not from despair, but because confronting mortality directly has sharpened how he spends time with his seven-year-old son, picking him up from school daily rather than after-care. He names his own psychological style directly, tying a real, self-diagnosed 'defensive pessimism' to his Ukrainian heritage and its long history of great-power conflict."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Luke Kemp's real first TGS episode (#153, directly cited here; his second appearance, #194, is already built in this graph — dct:references deliberately not asserted to #153 since it doesn't exist as an individual yet); Sheldon Solomon (real TGS episode #199, directly cited for Terror Management Theory), now built (2026-09-02). Nate Soares, also directly cited here, is now built (2026-09-02). Nate's own real academic paper, 'Economics for the future — Beyond the superorganism,' is directly cited again here (independently confirmed via multiple other episodes this session) — still not modeled as its own Work individual, consistent with established practice."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_216_SamHarris a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Navigating the Metacrisis: Finding Calm in the Storm through Awareness and Meditation"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real first conversation with Sam Harris, opening with Harris's own real, direct correction of a common misconception: mindfulness is not a technique for dampening fear and anxiety, since that framing still treats the feeling as an enemy to be managed. Genuine mindfulness means becoming willing to feel the raw sensation directly, watching thoughts arise and pass, which — even occasionally — reveals that real ease is possible beneath the habitual reactivity most people mistake for their baseline state."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-02-18"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-08"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.SamHarris ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/216-sam-harris> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=juOzBh2ODt0> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/TGS-216-Sam-Harris-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.DarkTriad ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.WakingUp, tgs:Interview.TGS_191_TaylorGuthrie ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Harris directly argues that virtually all human suffering traces to one source: the mind's largely unnoticed, incessant identification with thought — arguing this same mechanism operates identically whether the underlying threat is a personal cancer diagnosis or civilizational overshoot, since in both cases the real question is how much unnecessary suffering gets carried between now and whatever comes next. A real, direct, structural argument closes his systems-versus-individual-transformation point: rather than hoping for mass personal enlightenment, Harris argues for building institutions and incentive structures that make it easy for 'ordinary, neurotic people' — and even genuinely bad actors — to behave well by default, since currently the incentives are tuned so that behaving decently requires being a moral hero. He's real and direct that meaningful change doesn't require universal buy-in: a small number of people reaching genuinely actionable solutions and institutional leverage can move faster than waiting for broad consensus. A real, extended digression on animal cognition and consciousness — tool use, culture, self-awareness, language, art, and ritual behavior all being found, in some form, outside humans — is offered directly as evidence against treating human cognitive distinctiveness as a clean, categorical line rather than a matter of degree. Closes with real, direct personal candor about his own current concern: looking 18 months ahead, Harris says he'd want to track how AI and social media are supercharging political hyperpolarization and social fragmentation — setting existential AI risk aside entirely — worried people are losing the ability to even agree on a shared reality to argue about."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Robert Sapolsky (real TGS episode #88, directly cross-referenced, not built); Luke Kemp's real first TGS appearance (#153, directly cross-referenced regarding existential risk — already independently known as a real, flagged gap elsewhere in this graph, since only his second appearance, #194, is built); this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic (entropy, enlightenment, animal consciousness) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_217_WisdomInAWorldInCrisis a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
