tgs:Interview.TGS_218_RoryJohnston a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-08"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "This War Changes Everything: Are We Ready for Energy Shockwaves From the Strait of Hormuz?"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with oil market researcher Rory Johnston, founder of Commodity Context (already built), examining the real, unfolding 2026 Strait of Hormuz closure as, by Johnston's own real, direct account, the largest oil supply shock in the market's own history — more than half a billion barrels of expected production simply not materializing so far. Over three-quarters of the world's current population has never lived through a major global energy crisis of this scale, making this a genuinely novel, lived experience for most people alive today."@en ;
    thinkr:hasHostIntroduction "Today I'm joined by oil market researcher Rory Johnston, to discuss the large scale implications of the Strait of Hormuz closure for the long term and near term stability of global oil supply and the subsequent ripple effects on our industrial and geopolitical and financial systems. Rory Johnston is the founder of Commodity Context, also a lecturer at the University of Toronto's Munk School of Global Affairs and Public Policy, as well as the host of the Oil Ground Up podcast. Additionally, Rory is a fellow with both the Canadian Global Affairs Institute and the Payne Institute for Public Policy at the Colorado School of Mines. Prior to founding Commodity Context, Rory led commodity economics research at Scotiabank where he set the bank's energy and metals price forecasts, advised the bank's executives and clients, and sat on the bank's senior credit committee for commodity exposed sectors. This was a whirlwind conversation on the implications to the oil markets for the Strait of Hormuz situation. We talked about the impacts on California, the impacts on North America, the impacts on less developed countries, and the best case, worst case, base case scenario of the unfolding situation in Iran, in the Strait of Hormuz. I actually learned a lot on this episode and it reminded me of way back in the day, diving deep into the oil market particulars. I think you'll enjoy this episode. Please welcome Rory Johnston."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-04-23"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-29"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EconomicsAndMonetary ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.RoryJohnston ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/218-rory-johnston> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yriAdtE2hvg> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/TGS-218-Rory-Johnston-Transcript.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CrudeOil ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_61_TheStraitOfHormuzAndTheSpice, tgs:Organization.CommodityContext ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Johnston directly grounds the crisis in real, precise flow numbers: global oil demand sat around 105 million barrels a day before the Iran war, with roughly 20% of global energy passing through the Strait of Hormuz specifically — losses to date represent the largest real supply shock in the oil market's own recorded history. He's real and direct about a genuine structural lag: the closure itself, the physical unavailability of oil, and the market's own price response don't move in lockstep, creating a real, dangerous window where visible prices can understate the true severity of what's actually happening to supply. A real, striking historical parallel anchors his own analysis: he directly credits Winston Churchill's real, historic decision to convert the Royal Navy from Welsh coal to Persian oil as the actual founding moment of the modern geopolitical entanglement between Western power and Middle Eastern oil, tracing a direct line from that decision to the present crisis. Real, current, still-unfolding events are directly incorporated: Saudi Arabia's East-West Pipeline is documented as running at increased capacity specifically to bypass the Strait, and the show's own real, dated show-notes annotation records that Iran offered to reopen the Strait shortly after this conversation was recorded, contingent on the war itself ending. A real, self-deprecating personal moment closes the conversation: Johnston's own real, unhedged magic-wand answer — oil, gas, and coal with no emissions or downsides at all — followed directly by a real, half-joking worry that discussing his own diesel car purchase on air might have jinxed a ceasefire the following week."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: Daniel Yergin (real, directly cited author of The Prize, not built); Mark Carney (real, directly cited, his own Davos speech on ruptured international trust, not built given his role here as a cited public figure rather than a TGS guest); this episode's own citation list is otherwise extraordinarily dense with real, current geopolitical and market reporting rather than pointing to further individuals for this graph's own purposes."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_219_TadPatzek a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-04"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "Why Each American Lives Like a 40-Ton Whale: Power, Overshoot, and Climate"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate's real, direct first conversation with his own real, ~20-year friend Tad Patzek, thermodynamicist and earth scientist, opening with Patzek's own real, central argument: public discourse about energy operates almost entirely at the level of policy, politics, and moral aspiration, while remaining dangerously detached from the underlying physical infrastructure actually moving power and materials — and by the time the resulting biosphere damage becomes visible, it's already structurally locked in. Patzek's own real, vivid central image: an average American continuously consumes roughly 10,000 watts of power, the real metabolic equivalent of a 40-ton sperm whale, against a human body's own real baseline output of about 100 watts."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.TadPatzek ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/219-tad-patzek> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=BXR94APZ31Q> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/TGS-219-Tad-Patzek-Transcript-1.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Work.ThermalPowerAndClimateChange, tgs:Work.DrillingDown, tgs:Interview.TGS_27_JoeTainter ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Patzek directly frames climate change and overpopulation as downstream consequences of one real, underlying variable: the sheer surplus energy available to humanity, not as separate crises with separate causes. A real, historical throughline anchors his 'energy transitions are additive, not subtractive' argument: England's real, near-total dependence on coal by the 1800s directly parallels the US's real dependence on fossil fuels by 2010 — in his own real, direct account, every subsequent energy source in history has been layered on top of prior ones, increasing total power flowing through the system, rather than genuinely replacing what came before, meaning current renewable buildout should be expected to follow the same real, additive pattern rather than a clean substitution. He directly credits his own real, co-authored book with Joseph Tainter (already built), examining the Deepwater Horizon disaster, as the direct intellectual bridge between his own thermodynamic focus and Tainter's own real theory of societal collapse via diminishing returns on complexity. A real, extended, direct treatment of AI closes the conversation's contemporary-relevance section: Patzek discusses AI's real, substantial energy demand (individual data centers drawing 100-300 megawatts each), its real embodied-materials footprint, and a real, direct concern about AI being used by elites to further concentrate political power and centralize decision-making — treating AI's environmental and political risks as structurally linked, not separate concerns. Real, striking historical scale comparisons close the geopolitical section: the Roman Empire's own real historical geographic extent, and just-in-time global supply chains' real vulnerability as a single point of failure, both offered as concrete precedent for how complex systems built on surplus energy eventually confront real, hard limits. Closes with real, direct practical advice rather than abstract theory: learn real science through free resources like Khan Academy, deliberately practice manual skills and making things, and — Patzek's own real, direct parting request — download his own free, hyperlinked PDF book and follow its links at one's own pace rather than digest it linearly."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise almost entirely encyclopedic/institutional (thermodynamics, exponential growth, degrowth) rather than pointing to further individuals — Joseph Tainter is directly cross-referenced and already built, not a new find."@en ;
    .


tgs:Interview.TGS_220_AWorldOnThePrecipice a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Interview ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.MainShow ;
    skos:prefLabel "A World on the Precipice"@en ;
    skos:definition "Berman's return appearance amid the real-time Iran War/Strait of Hormuz oil crisis (roughly 11% of global oil supply offline at recording) — a systems-level breakdown of the crisis's already-locked-in effects (supply chain lags, refinery/tanker logistics, price cascades) regardless of how the war itself resolves, tying the acute shock back to the same longer-run themes (Peak Oil, the Carbon Pulse, the Superorganism, energy blindness) from his 4 prior TGS appearances."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-05-06"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-05-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasGuest tgs:Persona.ArtBerman ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/episode/220-art-berman> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.EnergyBlindness, tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.GreatSimplification, tgs:Concept.PeakOil, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Interview.TGS_101_ShaleOilAndTheSlurpingSound, tgs:Interview.TGS_92_BricsStrategicPetroleumReserveMetaphysics, tgs:Interview.TGS_54_TheStealthAdjustmentOfOil, tgs:Interview.TGS_3_OilBestAndWorstOfFuels ;
    skos:scopeNote "2026-08-13: real dates confirmed from a complete real transcript + show notes upload, resolving a prior discrepancy — the stub's own inferred issued date (2026-05-13) turned out correct, and the created date (2026-05-06) now matches what the existing bio-intro-pilot Evidence had already found independently. discusses/dct:references added from the same source. Real, further cross-references confirmed but deliberately NOT chased down in this pass: 2 more Reality Roundtable appearances (RR-1 'Electric Vehicles', RR-16 'The Mad Scramble for Power'), and a real Rory Johnston episode (TGS #218) cited directly — none built as individuals here, flagged for a future pass."@en ;
    .


